</h2></div><div><h4 id=3D"m3-sparse-attention" class=3D"font-inter font-med=
ium text-headline text-rich-h4"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">M3 sparse a=
ttention</strong></h4></div><p class=3D"">M3 attention is blockwise sparse =
on top of grouped-query attention (GQA): the KV sequence is partitioned int=
o fixed 128-token blocks, a lightweight index branch scores those blocks an=
d selects the topK (=3D 16) highest-scoring blocks for each GQA group, and =
a main branch then computes exact attention over only the selected blocks. =
Shapes: Hq =3D 64, Hkv =3D 4 (GQA factor Hq/Hkv =3D 16), D =3D 128, block =
=3D 128. The algorithm and the KV-stationary execution idea are from MiniMa=
x (<a rel=3D"noreferrer noopener" target=3D"_blank" href=3D"https://arxiv.o=
rg/abs/2606.13392" class=3D"text-purple-400 transition-colors hover:text-pu=
rple-500 focus-outline-primary dark:text-purple-50 dark:hover:text-purple-2=
5">paper</a>, <a rel=3D"noreferrer noopener" target=3D"_blank" href=3D"http=
s://github.com/MiniMax-AI/MSA" class=3D"text-purple-400 transition-colors h=
over:text-purple-500 focus-outline-primary dark:text-purple-50 dark:hover:t=
ext-purple-25">MSA kernels</a>).</p><div><h4 id=3D"q-outer-vs-kv-outer" cla=
ss=3D"font-inter font-medium text-headline text-rich-h4"><strong class=3D"f=
ont-semibold">Q-outer vs KV-outer</strong></h4></div><p class=3D"">A tradit=
ional dense FlashAttention-style kernel (e.g. FlashAttention4, aka FA4) loo=
ps Q-outer / K-inner: one threadblock owns a 128-row Q tile and streams all=
 KV through it at a 128=C3=97128 MMA. (128 is the common block/tile size we=
 use as the running example; other block sizes are valid.) As a result each=
 KV load is shared across the tile=E2=80=99s 128 Q tokens, and the MMA runs=
 at a large, efficient 128=C3=97128 tile.</p><p class=3D"">Sparsity, on the=
 other hand, admits two kernel structures:</p><ul class=3D"flex flex-col ga=
p-2"><li class=3D"flex items-start text-left [&amp;_ol]:mt-2 [&amp;_ul]:mt-=
2"><span class=3D"mr-3 text-purple-400">=E2=80=A2</span><span class=3D"flex=
-1"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">Q-outer (query-stationary).</strong> Ke=
ep Q-outer/K-inner; each query reads only its selected KV blocks. The M dim=
ension collapses to the GQA factor (16), so the MMA tile is <strong class=
=3D"font-semibold">16=C3=97128</strong> =E2=80=94 decode-shaped, and natura=
lly served by, e.g., a FlashInfer decode attention kernel. Tensor cores are=
 underfed, and a KV block selected by M queries is read M times repeatedly.=
</span></li><li class=3D"flex items-start text-left [&amp;_ol]:mt-2 [&amp;_=
ul]:mt-2"><span class=3D"mr-3 text-purple-400">=E2=80=A2</span><span class=
=3D"flex-1"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">KV-outer (KV-stationary).</stro=
ng> Invert the map: for each (kv_head, kv_block), gather the queries that s=
elected it, load the KV block once, and run a full <strong class=3D"font-se=
mibold">128=C3=97128</strong> tile, emitting one partial (O_partial, m, l) =
per (query, kv_block). So each Q token writes up to topK (=3D 16) partial-O=
 blocks, which a following combine kernel LSE-merges into its final output.=
 Full tiles; each KV block read once.</span></li></ul><figure class=3D"flex=
