div><div><h4 id=3D"attention-store-optimization-from-attn-scattered-writes-=
to-combine-gathered-read" class=3D"font-inter font-medium text-headline tex=
t-rich-h4"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">Attention store optimization: fr=
om attn scattered writes to combine gathered read</strong></h4></div><p cla=
ss=3D"">The attention kernel writes partial-O in contiguous blocks instead =
of scattered writes; the combine kernel does gathered loads. A KV-outer CTA=
 serves a scattered set of queries, so the natural store would scatter each=
 partial back to its query=E2=80=99s slot. But scattered writes inside the =
attention kernel are instruction-latency-sensitive and easily stall the who=
le pipeline. The combine kernel, by contrast, is memory-bandwidth bound and=
 far less sensitive to load-instruction latency, so a gather costs little t=
here. We therefore let the attention kernel write partial-O in contiguous, =
tile-ordered blocks (a single bulk-TMA pass, pure address arithmetic), and =
build an index from (q_idx, kv_head, top-i) =E2=86=92 (kv_block_idx, offset=
_in_kv_block) that the combine kernel gathers through.</p><div><h4 id=3D"at=
tention-load-optimization-3-warp-gathered-reads" class=3D"font-inter font-m=
edium text-headline text-rich-h4"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">Attention=
 load optimization: 3-warp gathered reads</strong></h4></div><p class=3D"">=
Queries are gathered by 3 warps via cp.async, head-packed, with the index r=
ead overlapped. The gather lands directly in the swizzled MMA-A layout. Pac=
king each query=E2=80=99s GQA-head group into one =E2=80=9Cbox=E2=80=9D yie=
lds one index read per query-group instead of per row, and the Q index gath=
er is overlapped with the previous tile=E2=80=99s Q load (a one-tile softwa=
re pipeline).</p><div><h4 id=3D"attention-pipeline-optimization-delete-the-=
softmaxstore-sync" class=3D"font-inter font-medium text-headline text-rich-=
h4"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">Attention pipeline optimization: delete=
 the softmax=E2=86=92store sync</strong></h4></div><p class=3D"">The softma=
x warpgroup writes out the (m, l) stats (row max and row sum) directly. Sin=
ce O is never rescaled in the KV outer attention kernel, the store warpgrou=
p no longer depends on the softmax warpgroup =E2=80=94 it just evacuates th=
e raw PV accumulator =E2=80=94 which shortens the pipeline.</p><div><h4 id=
=3D"load-balanced-split-q-scheduling--persistent-kernel" class=3D"font-inte=
r font-medium text-headline text-rich-h4"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">L=
oad-balanced split-Q scheduling + persistent kernel</strong></h4></div><p c=
lass=3D"">A heavy KV block is split across CTAs by a deterministic, atomic-=
free scheduler. A single on-device kernel flattens all (kv_head, kv_block, =
query) work into one sequence and assigns each CTA a contiguous slice. The =
attention kernel is persistent (grid =E2=89=88 #SMs), overlapping each bloc=
k=E2=80=99s store with the next block=E2=80=99s load.</p><div><h4 id=3D"d2h=
-elimination-fixed-per-request-shapes" class=3D"font-inter font-medium text=
-headline text-rich-h4"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">D2H elimination: fi=
xed per-request shapes</strong></h4></div><p class=3D"">The selected-block =
count and the KV-block=E2=86=92Q-token mapping are dynamic shapes that vary=
 per layer. Reading them back to size kernel grids would force a device-to-=
host (D2H) copy on every layer. We instead fix all tensor shapes per reques=
t =E2=80=94 aligned to q_len, max_kv_blocks, num_splits =E2=80=94 and skip =
the padded compute; with the persistent kernel, the scheduler always output=
s a fixed N =C3=97 num_sms work items. The result: no D2H sync.</p><div><h4=
 id=3D"c-aot-dispatch-backend" class=3D"font-inter font-medium text-headlin=
e text-rich-h4"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">C++ AOT dispatch backend</s=
trong></h4></div><p class=3D"">The kernels are AOT-exported per config and =
driven from a C++ op. KV-outer is several small kernels, and launching each=
 from Python/CuTe-DSL adds host-side overhead between launches that makes u=
p most of the end-to-end (wall-clock) latency even though the GPU work is s=
mall. Dispatching the precompiled kernels from a C++ op removes that launch=
 overhead, and is useful in cases where CUDA graphs are hard to apply.</p><=
hr class=3D"my-4 border-dashed border-mud-500"><div><h2 id=3D"4-differences=
-from-open-source-msa" class=3D"font-inter font-medium text-headline text-r=
ich-h2"><strong class=3D"font-semibold">4. Differences from open-source MSA=
</strong></h2></div><p class=3D"">Our kernel follows MiniMax=E2=80=99s KV-o=
uter design, but the kernel itself was written independently, before MSA wa=
s public =E2=80=94 so the two are separate implementations of the same idea=
 rather than a before/after of one codebase. The comparison here is a rough=
 study of the two implementations.</p><p class=3D"">The major difference is=
 in the attention kernel=E2=80=99s load and store paths. On the store side,=
 open-source MSA scatters each partial to its (query, rank) slot inside the=
 attention epilogue and normalizes it there, while we store partial outputs=
 tile-ordered and raw and defer the scatter and normalization to the combin=
e kernel. The two also organize warps differently: MSA uses a single shared=
 4-warp Q-load/epilogue warpgroup for both the query load and the output st=
ore, whereas we use separate warps =E2=80=94 a 3-warp cp.async gather for t=
he load, a 4-warp group for the tmem=E2=86=92rmem=E2=86=92smem evacuation o=
f the PV accumulator, and a single TMA store per Q block for the final writ=
e.</p><hr class=3D"my-4 border-dashed border-mud-500"><div><h2 id=3D"5-resu=
lts" class=3D"font-inter font-medium text-headline text-rich-h2"><strong cl=
ass=3D"font-semibold">5. Results</strong></h2></div><p class=3D""><strong c=
lass=3D"font-semibold">Attention-kernel throughput and HBM bandwidth.</stro=
ng> We compare three backends on a single B200 (fp8): Q-outer (FlashInfer),=
 MSA (MiniMax=E2=80=99s open-source kernel), and our KV-outer kernel. Acros=
s every shape and reuse level, KV-outer leads in attention-kernel throughpu=
t =E2=80=94 peaking near <strong class=3D"font-semibold">980 TFLOP/s</stron=
g>, roughly <strong class=3D"font-semibold">1.9=E2=80=932.4=C3=97 the Q-out=
er (FlashInfer) baseline and ~1.6=C3=97 open-source MSA</strong>. KV-outer =
(and MSA, also KV-stationary) are HBM-bound, while the query-stationary bas=
eline is L2-bound and barely touches HBM.</p><figure class=3D"flex flex-col=
